WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.095
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:08.955
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.255 --> 00:00:15.716
Unit twenty-two: Drama and Performance. Today you will add emphasis with 'only when / only in' inversion.

4
00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:18.726
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.714
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:24.365
Drama.

7
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.464
Drama means a story written to be performed by actors.

8
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.549
Greek drama was performed at festivals.

9
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.500
Your turn. Say: Drama.

10
00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:38.124
Drama.

11
00:00:38.883 --> 00:00:39.989
Script.

12
00:00:40.983 --> 00:00:43.540
Script means the written text of a play or film.

13
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.985
The script includes detailed stage directions.

14
00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:50.387
Your turn. Say: Script.

15
00:00:53.233 --> 00:00:53.979
Script.

16
00:00:54.717 --> 00:00:55.578
Stage.

17
00:00:56.583 --> 00:00:59.581
Stage means the raised area where actors perform.

18
00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:01.939
The stage was almost bare.

19
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.842
Your turn. Say: Stage.

20
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.678
Stage.

21
00:01:09.417 --> 00:01:10.441
Dialogue.

22
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.518
Dialogue means conversation between characters.

23
00:01:15.117 --> 00:01:17.023
The dialogue is sharp and funny.

24
00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:20.292
Your turn. Say: Dialogue.

25
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:24.227
Dialogue.

26
00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:25.845
Tension.

27
00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.627
Tension means a feeling of suspense or conflict.

28
00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:32.228
The silence increased the tension.

29
00:01:32.817 --> 00:01:35.144
Your turn. Say: Tension.

30
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:38.908
Tension.

31
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:40.674
Audience.

32
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:44.345
Audience means the people watching a performance.

33
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.031
The audience gasped at the twist.

34
00:01:47.617 --> 00:01:50.083
Your turn. Say: Audience.

35
00:01:52.933 --> 00:01:53.992
Audience.

36
00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:55.856
Performance.

37
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.569
Performance means the act of presenting a play.

38
00:02:00.183 --> 00:02:02.520
Her performance won several awards.

39
00:02:03.117 --> 00:02:06.082
Your turn. Say: Performance.

40
00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:10.027
Performance.

41
00:02:10.783 --> 00:02:11.703
Director.

42
00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:16.063
Director means the person who guides the actors and the production.

43
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.451
The director reimagined the play in modern Phnom Penh.

44
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.482
Your turn. Say: Director.

45
00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:26.358
Director.

46
00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:31.061
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:36.345
Visal, you read the play first and then saw it performed. Which did you prefer?

48
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:46.859
Definitely the performance. Only in performance does the play come alive; on the page, the long silences seemed empty.

49
00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:49.427
What changed in the theatre?

50
00:02:50.517 --> 00:02:59.404
The tension. Only when the actors held those silences did I realise how much the characters were hiding from each other.

51
00:03:00.517 --> 00:03:04.327
So the director plays a creative role, not just a technical one.

52
00:03:05.417 --> 00:03:12.539
Absolutely. Not until I saw it did I understand that every script is incomplete without interpretation.

53
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.467
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:16.317 --> 00:03:18.990
Grammar focus: Only when inversion.

55
00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.453
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.811
Only in performance does the play come alive.

57
00:03:24.717 --> 00:03:28.469
Only when the actors held the silences did I realise what was hidden.

58
00:03:29.383 --> 00:03:32.045
Not until I saw it did I understand the script.

59
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:45.347
After 'Only when / Only in / Only after / Not until' at the start, invert the MAIN clause: auxiliary + subject + verb. The first clause keeps normal order: 'Only when the actors held… did I realise'.

60
00:03:46.083 --> 00:03:50.558
Be careful! Don't say: Only in performance the play comes alive.

61
00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:56.067
Say: Only in performance does the play come alive.

62
00:03:56.367 --> 00:03:59.086
Invert after 'Only in…': does + subject.

63
00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:02.564
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:04.902
From Page to Stage.

65
00:04:05.202 --> 00:04:08.769
A play script is, in many ways, an unfinished work of art.

66
00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:14.936
Only when it is performed do its pauses, gestures and rhythms take on their full meaning.

67
00:04:15.933 --> 00:04:20.754
Directors make countless decisions that the script leaves open, from casting to lighting and pacing.

68
00:04:21.767 --> 00:04:26.297
As a result, the same play can feel comic in one production and tragic in another.

69
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.922
Not until we see drama as a collaboration between writer, actors and audience can we fully appreciate it.

70
00:04:34.033 --> 00:04:38.081
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:38.917 --> 00:04:41.032
Only in performance blank alive.

72
00:04:41.332 --> 00:04:46.031
A, the play comes. B, does the play come. C, comes the play does.

73
00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:52.135
The answer is B: does the play come.

74
00:04:53.283 --> 00:04:55.434
Not until I saw it blank the ending.

75
00:04:55.734 --> 00:05:00.328
A, did I understand. B, I understood. C, I did understand.

76
00:05:03.867 --> 00:05:06.946
The answer is A: did I understand.

77
00:05:08.083 --> 00:05:10.524
The person who guides the actors is the blank.

78
00:05:10.824 --> 00:05:14.628
A, director. B, audience. C, script.

79
00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:19.934
The answer is A: director.

80
00:05:21.083 --> 00:05:22.990
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:28.244
A, build tension. B, do tension. C, make up tension.

82
00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.055
The answer is A: build tension.

83
00:05:35.217 --> 00:05:37.936
According to the reading, a play script is…

84
00:05:38.236 --> 00:05:43.654
A, a finished work. B, an unfinished work of art. C, less important than lighting.

85
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.186
The answer is B: an unfinished work of art.

86
00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:54.633
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:55.483 --> 00:05:58.261
How does watching a play differ from reading its script?

88
00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:16.608
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:16.908 --> 00:06:51.403
Watching a play differs from reading it because performance adds voice, movement and timing, which the script can only suggest. Only when I hear an actor's tone do I fully understand whether a line is sincere or sarcastic. For example, a silence that looks empty on the page can feel unbearable on stage. Some readers enjoy scripts more because they can imagine the scenes freely. Still, I believe drama is written to be seen and shared with an audience.

90
00:06:51.703 --> 00:06:53.378
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:54.283 --> 00:06:58.248
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and only when inversion.

92
00:06:58.548 --> 00:07:02.338
Today's value is teamwork. We can do more when we work together.

93
00:07:02.638 --> 00:07:05.836
See you in Unit twenty-three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:06.136 --> 00:07:09.090
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

